nce of the drug in this population to prevent adverse outcomes such as neurotoxicity or exacerbated nephrotoxicity [Data: Entities (1256, 14727); Relationships (6445, 6446)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Even renally healthy young adults may face risks with acyclovir use\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Surprisingly, acyclovir is also flagged as contraindicated for '肾功能良好的青壮年' (young adults with good renal function), based on the same evidence citing prolonged half-life in renal impairment. While this appears contradictory at first glance, the underlying rationale likely stems from the general precaution to exclude any patient with undiagnosed or subclinical cardiac or renal conditions before initiating therapy. The cited evidence emphasizes screening for 'severe heart or kidney disease' prior to use, suggesting that even seemingly healthy individuals require evaluation due to the drug’s narrow safety margin in vulnerable physiological states [Data: Entities (14973); Relationships (6447)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Renal excretion is the primary route of acyclovir elimination, driving toxicity risks\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Acyclovir is predominantly excreted unchanged via the kidneys, making renal function a critical determinant of its safety profile. Tissue distribution studies in animals show the highest drug concentrations in the kidneys, followed by the intestines, lungs, liver, and plasma. This renal tropism contributes to crystal deposition in tubules and functional decline over time—as evidenced by rising drug concentrations on day 18 versus day 1 of a prolonged regimen in bone marrow transplant patients, indicating progressive reduction in renal clearance. This accumulation likely explains the increased incidence of adverse effects with extended use [Data: Relationships (12193, 12194); Entities (5759)].\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 7.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity